## Main mathematical attack

I attempted to break the rank-one/rank-two threshold at zero, on unequal cuts, and at full rank. It survives with p,q>=1 and a fixed cut. The zero matrix is detected by the total count; nonzero rank-one matrices are rectangles; opposite parity on the larger block gives sharpness. Global parity and its complement have rank exactly two across every nontrivial cut and agree on every proper marginal.

The independent enumeration covers the relevant finite classes and supports, rather than substitutes for, these proofs. It is especially useful that the 4x4 rank<=2 class is exhaustively filtered rather than generated from a potentially incomplete family of factorizations.

## Why the rank claim is not uniquely GF(2)

The same two parity matrices are unions of two disjoint rectangles. They therefore have rank two under the relevant real, nonnegative, and Boolean-OR conventions as well. Rank-one binary matrices remain rectangles. The reconstruction threshold is driven by the query family and parity, not a field-specific phenomenon. S8 underscores why rank conventions must be kept separate even where this example happens to agree.

The ordinary-real rank of a 0/1 inner-product matrix and the ordinary-real rank of its sign matrix are also not communication sign-rank. A matrix with small GF(2) rank need not have small real rank. Tensor results over integer or real arithmetic cannot automatically be transplanted to GF(2) cores.

## Profile and recompression checks

The count identity is correct: convert a bit to (1-sign)/2 and group factor states. The factor-only recompression proof is standard exact linear algebra and handles cancellation correctly when both factor ranks are considered. The complementary-row quotient bound follows from quotienting the row space by the all-ones line when that line is present.

The practical difficulty is not the final Walsh pairing. It is obtaining and maintaining the correct coordinate-restricted histograms. Their entries are integers whose bit lengths grow with cube size. A statement of O(r2^r) arithmetic work after histograms exist is not a total query complexity theorem and not O(r2^r) bit work without qualification.

## Representation attack

The inner-product example separates two fixed-cut explicit formats. It does not establish a separation from the best variable order, from a disjoint XOR of paired products, or from every tensor representation. This must be in the theorem statement or immediately adjacent, not buried in a limitation section.

## Disagreement and required repair

I give the exact rank-threshold corollary somewhat more expository value than Reviewer 1 does: it sharply prevents one tempting intuition. But I agree it is not yet a strong theorem-first contribution. Retain it as a carefully qualified lemma, prove a new coordinate-aware persistence result, and account for factor-basis gauge equivalence before making minimal-state or canonical-representation claims.
